        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "4181cf75-0cab-5f6c-8098-7c7f2b58b2c5",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Stefan Edberg, Édouard Roger-Vasselin, Brad Gilbert, Julien Benneteau\n- Sports: men's tennis, singles, doubles\n- Events: 1984 unofficial Olympic tournament, 1990 Thriftway ATP Championships, 2014 Roland Garros\n- Concepts: serve-and-volley style, world No. 1 rankings, Grand Slam titles, Davis Cup\n\nFacts:\n- Stefan Edberg won the unofficial Olympic tennis tournament in 1984.\n- Between 1985 and 1996, Stefan Edberg won six Grand Slam singles titles and three Grand Slam men's doubles titles.\n- Stefan Edberg won the Masters Grand Prix, four Masters Series titles, four Championship Series titles, and was part of four Swedish Davis Cup-winning teams.\n- In 1990, defending champion Brad Gilbert lost the Thriftway ATP Championships singles final to Stefan Edberg by 6–1, 6–1.\n- Stefan Edberg, born on 19 January 1966, is a Swedish former professional tennis player who reached world No. 1 in both singles and doubles.\n- Stefan Edberg was a major proponent of the serve-and-volley style, stayed in the singles top 10 for ten straight years and in the top 5 for nine years, and is considered one of the greatest players of his era.\n- In 2014, Édouard Roger-Vasselin and Julien Benneteau won the men's doubles title at Roland Garros.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "7aa786d0-348c-5681-8f66-33726a9e772a",
        "text": "Page: Stefan Edberg. Sentence: Stefan Bengt Edberg (] ; born 19 January 1966) is a Swedish former world no. 1 professional tennis player (in both singles and doubles).\nPage: Stefan Edberg. Sentence: A major proponent of the serve-and-volley style of tennis, he won six Grand Slam singles titles and three Grand Slam men's doubles titles between 1985 and 1996.\nPage: Stefan Edberg. Sentence: He also won the Masters Grand Prix and was a part of the Swedish Davis Cup-winning-team four times.\nPage: Stefan Edberg. Sentence: In addition he won four Masters Series titles, four Championship Series titles and the unofficial Olympic tournament 1984, was ranked in the singles top 10 for ten successive years, 9 years in the top 5, and is considered one of the greatest players of his era.\nPage: Édouard Roger-Vasselin. Sentence: He won the men's doubles title at Roland Garros in 2014, partnering Julien Benneteau.\nPage: 1990 Thriftway ATP Championships – Singles. Sentence: Brad Gilbert was the defending champion but Stefan Edberg defeated him 6–1, 6–1 in the final.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
      },
